# Linux Host fast-send qualification

Historical experiment; superseded on `raptorq-upgrade` by deletion of the
application datagram pacer and its platform feature switches. The shared Host
sender always retains the 1 Gbps budget floor and Quinn scheduling. Do not use
the old feature selections/comparison commands below for current builds; use
the release runbook and RaptorQ qualification record. Historical measurements
below remain historical, not acceptance evidence for the new combined build.

Branch: linux-fast-send. Baseline: published 1.0.137.
Candidate: 1.0.138-linux-fast-send. Built; not merged or live-accepted.

The operator subsequently requested inclusion in the combined
1.0.141-mouse-edge-recovery candidate with Client bounds, Mac logging and Linux
mouse-button cleanup. It is integrated there, not yet merged to main or
live-accepted. Prior isolated evidence below remains component evidence only.

## Scope

Apply the two accepted macOS Host sender-policy changes to Linux Host: bypass
the application DATAGRAM pacer and floor the rate input to the Quinn controller's
window calculation at 1,000,000,000 bits/s. This is transport headroom, not a
fixed encoder rate, measured capacity, unlimited queue, or strict wire-rate cap.
Quinn still schedules transmission; the existing RTT/window bounds remain.

Use the shared rate policy with a Linux-only compile selection. Do not change
the macOS feature selection or Client defaults. Linux does not enable macOS
source-first FEC, extra diagnostics, a new congestion controller, different
queues, or different MTU. No protocol/ABI or Client upgrade is required.
Keep the paced selection available for controlled comparison until acceptance;
do not add a user preference or global configuration key.

## Build and automated gates

- Linux Host package default: quinn-telemetry,linux-fast-send. The existing
  quinn-telemetry-only selection is the baseline, not the candidate default.
- Prove that slider changes still reach the encoder unchanged, budget flooring
  does not cap higher rates, windows remain RTT-bounded, and other platform/
  default-feature builds retain their prior policy.
- Rust transport unit tests, real encrypted native protocol loopback and C ABI
  active/setup-promoted loopback use the exact package's Cargo feature set.
  The loss fixture must select outgoing_pacer(), not force a paced connection.
  Candidate packaging also repeats unit/native loss tests with the paced
  baseline, then restores the selected archive for the C ABI checks.
- The receiver-side loopback proxy omits evenly distributed encrypted datagrams
  at 0/0.5/1/3/5%; check all 300 reconstructed synthetic frames byte-for-byte,
  in order, plus pre/post-FEC counters. This is not random WAN qualification.
- Hosted Rocky build uses existing pinned dependencies and the complete CUDA
  architecture set. Require package/feature-marker/version/provenance gates.
  Mac compile/tests guard unchanged behavior; no signed Mac package is needed.

## Live qualification before merge

Use an authorized, idle hardware test Host and an existing matching Client.
Do not interrupt an operator's live session or deploy to an End-User Client.
Record installed package hash/version and the sender policy startup marker.

Compare the released paced Host and the new candidate at the same profile,
resolution, content, encoder target and network route. Use ten-minute baseline
and candidate soaks at 150 Mbps, including moving footage and large keyframes.
Then inject loss at the receiver at 0.5/1/3/5%, removing it after each run and on
failure. Track sender queue evictions, frame delivery gaps, RTT, pre/post-FEC
loss, audio continuity and input/Wacom responsiveness. Repeat on constrained or
WAN paths: bypassing application pacing may move bursts/drops downstream.

No improvement claim from synthetic loopback alone. Keep this branch isolated
until live evidence supports merging; retain 1.0.137 as the rollback package.

## Automated evidence

Package-source root: e2f4af4e7fdaedd2c6869a253deea8ad2aa38e0e.
Hosted Linux Host run 35369233130 passed. Each policy passed 24 Rust unit tests,
the real encrypted lane loopback and the receiver-side controlled-loss matrix.
Each loss phase lasted about one second at approximately 150 Mbps; these are
regression fixtures, not ten-minute soaks or random WAN-loss qualification.

| Policy | Exact ordered frames | Source symbols | Missing before FEC | Unrecovered |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Fast-send candidate | 300/300 | 73,200 | 1,481 | 0 |
| Paced baseline | 300/300 | 73,200 | 1,423 | 0 |

Both C ABI trust paths passed video, audio, input, bidirectional control,
setup promotion and peer closure. The final archive and packaged Host were
verified as fast-send, not the baseline archive compiled for comparison.
RPM payload/log-directory/version/provenance checks passed. See HANDOFF for
the artifact path and checksum. Both Clients and Mac Host also compiled and
passed their existing gates at the unchanged runtime implementation commit.

Hardware playback, constrained/WAN paths, ten-minute soaks, large keyframe
bursts, audio/input/Wacom responsiveness and live loss tests remain pending.
No hardware package installation has occurred. Select an available test pair
with the operator before beginning those tests.
